# Copy to `stacks/sage/.env` in the root of the devbox deployment repo (the directory
# holding docker-compose.sh), not into this repo. `docker-compose.sh` passes it
# to every compose file with --env-file.
#
# Naming follows the devbox convention (see stacks/sanya/.env.example): a `Sage_`
# prefix, Capitalized words, acronyms left uppercase. These names exist only
# for compose interpolation — stacks/sage/compose.yaml maps them onto the
# SAGE_* variables the application itself reads (apps/api/src/core/config.ts).
# Renaming one of these is safe; renaming the SAGE_* side is not.
#
# An empty value means "not set" (#72). Compose substitutes an empty string
# for a variable this file omits, so the two are indistinguishable by the time
# the application reads them, and it treats both as "use the default". A value
# that IS set and malformed still stops the boot with the variable named --
# that is the point of the validation, not something the rule above weakens.
# The exception is secrets: an empty Sage_JWT_Secret or Sage_DB_Password fails
# to boot rather than falling back to anything.

# Absolute path to a checkout of this repository on the devbox. The compose
# file uses it as the Docker build context, because the Dockerfile's COPY
# paths (pnpm-workspace.yaml, apps/api/...) are relative to the repo root.
Sage_Host_Home_Dir=/home/ec2-user/dev/sage

Sage_Node_Env=production
Sage_API_Port=3210
Sage_Public_URL=https://api.sage.filinn.ai

# Browser origins allowed to call the API cross-origin (#133). The Mini App is
# served from its own host, so every request it makes is cross-origin and the
# interesting ones are preflighted — with no allow-list the browser refuses
# them before the API ever sees the signed `initData`, and the page reports a
# generic sign-in failure. Comma-separated, bare origins: no path and no
# trailing slash, or the boot stops (a trailing slash never matches the
# `Origin` header a browser sends, so it would look configured and match
# nothing). Leave unset to take the built-in default, which is exactly this.
Sage_WebApp_Origins=https://tg.sage.filinn.ai

# Postgres runs in the existing `postgres` container on the same network.
Sage_DB_Host=postgres
Sage_DB_Port=5432
Sage_DB_Name=sage
Sage_DB_User=sage
# Generate a real password before deploying — production config refuses to
# boot with the literal placeholder below (apps/api/src/core/config.ts).
Sage_DB_Password=change_me

# openssl rand -base64 48
# Generate a real secret before deploying — production config refuses to
# boot with the literal placeholder below, or with anything shorter than
# 32 characters (apps/api/src/core/config.ts, R-1c/R-14e).
Sage_JWT_Secret=change_me

Sage_Apple_Bundle_Id=ai.filinn.sage
Sage_Sentry_DSN=
# `log` is the only implemented sender in Phase 0 — magic-link mail is written
# to the log, not sent (backlog #21 tracks the real thing). `smtp` and `resend`
# are recognised names with no implementation behind them, so the API refuses
# to boot on either rather than failing on the first email a user waits for
# (apps/api/src/core/config.ts).
Sage_Mail_Sender=log
Sage_Log_Level=info

# Background worker. Phase 1 runs it inside the API process. To move it into
# its own container: set Sage_Worker_Inline=false here, and add a `sage-worker`
# service to stacks/sage/compose.yaml running `node dist/worker.js` with this
# same env file. No application code changes.
Sage_Worker_Inline=true
Sage_Worker_Concurrency=1
Sage_Worker_Poll_Ms=2000
Sage_Worker_Stale_After_Ms=600000

# LLM. claude_cli spends the owner's personal subscription (decisions.md §4,
# risk C1). anthropic_api is implemented but needs billing.
Sage_LLM_Provider=claude_cli
Sage_Claude_CLI_Path=claude
Sage_LLM_Model=claude-opus-5
Sage_LLM_Timeout_Ms=90000
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN is required by the CLI itself. Treat it as
# equivalent to subscription access: never log it, never commit it.
Sage_Claude_Code_OAuth_Token=

# Autonomy thresholds (FR-INT-005). Raise these after the first week of real
# use if the Inbox shows too many wrong auto-created objects — a higher bar
# trades convenience for trust, which is the right trade in that direction.
Sage_Autonomy_Task=0.80
Sage_Autonomy_Commitment=0.85
# Currently has no effect: D-013 (#71) routes every `tension` through
# confirmation regardless of confidence, because the model is confident about
# its false ones. Kept set so that lifting the suspension is one code change
# and not also a hunt for the missing variable.
Sage_Autonomy_Tension=0.80
Sage_Autonomy_Note=0.75

# Telegram (#61). One bot for everything: account linking, the Mini App probe,
# notifications later. Absent is a legitimate state — the Telegram routes
# answer 503 rather than accepting data they cannot verify.
#
# Sage_Telegram_Bot_Token is the key that signs every Mini App session: it is
# a SEC-002 secret, never logged and never committed. The webhook secret is
# whatever you pass to setWebhook as `secret_token`; without it the webhook
# would accept an update from anyone who learned the URL, and a forged update
# is a forged identity.
Sage_Telegram_Bot_Token=
Sage_Telegram_Bot_Username=
Sage_Telegram_Webhook_Secret=

# Langfuse Cloud, EU region (D-011). Traces carry the FULL prompt and reply,
# i.e. the text of captures — a deliberate exception taken by the owner, scoped
# to Langfuse only: Sentry, pino and every other log sink keep redacting.
# Tokens never travel to it (SEC-009). Leave the keys empty and the tracer is
# a no-op — traces are observability, and their absence must never be a reason
# a capture goes uninterpreted.
Sage_Langfuse_Public_Key=
Sage_Langfuse_Secret_Key=
Sage_Langfuse_Base_Url=https://cloud.langfuse.com

# --- Object storage: original audio of voice captures (Phase 2) -------------
# `fake` is an in-memory store: the API runs without credentials and no test
# can write to the bucket. Set to `r2` and fill all four values below; the API
# refuses to boot naming whichever one is missing. A misspelled driver stops
# the boot too, rather than becoming `undefined` in the worker.
Sage_Object_Store=fake
Sage_R2_Account_ID=
Sage_R2_Bucket=
Sage_R2_Access_Key_ID=
# Never logged (SEC-008): this is the key to every recording ever made.
Sage_R2_Secret_Access_Key=
# SEC-003. An empty value means 30, not zero -- zero days would make the next
# purge run delete every recording.
Sage_Audio_Retention_Days=30

# --- Speech to text (Phase 2) ----------------------------------------------
# `fake` returns an empty transcript and costs nothing. Set to `whisper_api`
# and provide the key; a misspelled provider stops the boot.
Sage_STT_Provider=fake
# Never logged (SEC-008).
Sage_OpenAI_API_Key=
# whisper-1 and not gpt-4o-transcribe: `verbose_json` with per-segment
# confidence is whisper-1 only. See backlog #75 before changing this.
Sage_STT_Model=whisper-1
# Stated, never detected: detection on a short Russian note is a coin flip.
Sage_STT_Language=ru
# FR-VOICE-006: below this, nothing from the capture auto-applies.
#
# 0.4, and the number only means anything next to the scale it is read
# against. `no_speech_prob` is a GATE, not a factor (#92): confidence is
# `exp(avg_logprob)` weighted by segment duration, and a recording whose
# weighted `no_speech_prob` exceeds 0.6 scores exactly 0. On that scale clean
# speech measures ~0.73 and mumbling ~0.20, so 0.4 sits in the gap.
#
# It was 0.6 under the old product formula, where clean SYNTHESIZED speech
# scored 0.618 -- a margin of 0.018, and real dictation in a car would have
# been below it every time. Do not carry 0.6 back without changing the
# formula back too.
#
# Still due for calibration against `transcription_runs.confidence` next to
# `transcript_edited_at` after a couple of weeks of real use.
Sage_STT_Min_Confidence=0.4

# --- Calendars (Phase 3) ---------------------------------------------------
# Which providers are offered on the connections page. Comma-separated,
# `fake` by default -- the same rule as the object store and STT: a
# deployment with no OAuth application still boots, and nothing can reach a
# real Google account by accident. A misspelled name stops the boot.
Sage_Calendar_Providers=fake
# The Google OAuth application (Cloud console, External consent screen).
# Scopes, the narrowest pair that covers the two calls the provider makes
# (calendarList.list and events.list) -- both sensitive, neither restricted, so
# no third-party security audit is required:
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.calendarlist.readonly
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events.readonly
# The broader calendar.readonly is deliberately NOT used: it would also grant
# calendar settings and ACLs, which nothing reads.
# Redirect URI, registered exactly once:
#   ${Sage_Public_URL}/connections/google/callback
Sage_Google_Client_Id=
# Never logged (SEC-002, denylist entry `client_secret`).
Sage_Google_Client_Secret=
# The Microsoft app registration in the twelvedevs.com tenant. Delegated
# Calendars.Read + offline_access + User.Read, admin consent granted by the
# owner. Redirect URI: ${Sage_Public_URL}/connections/microsoft/callback
Sage_MS_Client_Id=
Sage_MS_Client_Secret=
Sage_MS_Tenant_Id=
# AES-256-GCM key for provider refresh tokens (spec §3.1). 32 bytes, base64:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# REQUIRED as soon as Sage_Calendar_Providers names google or microsoft -- a
# deployment able to hold a months-long Google refresh token must not be able
# to start without the means to encrypt it. There is deliberately no default:
# a fallback key would mean tokens sealed under a value published in the
# repository, which looks encrypted in a dump and is not. A malformed value
# stops the boot in every case, including `fake`.
#
# Rotating it means bumping `key_version` in code and re-sealing the existing
# rows; changing it alone makes every connection undecryptable, and the only
# symptom is the provider refusing requests days later.
Sage_Token_Encryption_Key=
# How often each connection re-syncs its window. The job reschedules itself,
# so this is a period and not a cron expression.
Sage_Calendar_Sync_Interval_Minutes=15
# The sliding window, in local days around today. It is overwritten on every
# sync -- events older than the left edge are gone for good, which is why
# `calendar_day_load` keeps a daily snapshot of the metrics.
Sage_Calendar_Window_Past_Days=7
Sage_Calendar_Window_Future_Days=30
# The working day, used for "largest free block" and the fragmentation index
# (spec §9). A STATED v0 heuristic (P-08), not a claim about when the owner
# works -- it lives here precisely so it can be corrected without touching
# code. HH:MM, 24-hour; start must be earlier than end or the boot fails.
Sage_Day_Window_Start=09:00
Sage_Day_Window_End=19:00
